@startuml mtga-go-concurrency
title Bounded Go Pipeline - Catalog Publication
left to right direction
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam componentStyle rectangle

database "PostgreSQL\ndurable jobs + lease" as Jobs
component "Run owner\ncontext + deadline + errgroup" as Owner
queue "provider jobs\ncapacity: P" as ProviderJobs
component "fetch pool\nlimit: provider budget" as Fetch
queue "observations\ncapacity: O" as Observations
component "normalize pool\nlimit: CPU budget" as Normalize
queue "normalized cards\ncapacity: N" as Normalized
component "stable reducer\nsingle owner" as Reducer
component "batch writer\nlimit: pgxpool budget" as Writer
database "PostgreSQL\nversioned catalog" as Catalog
component "immutable object writer\nlimit: storage budget" as ObjectWriter
cloud "Object storage / CDN" as Storage
component "hash + manifest signer" as Signer
component "atomic activator" as Activator

Jobs --> Owner : claim lease + idempotency key
Owner --> ProviderJobs : enqueue approved providers
ProviderJobs --> Fetch
Fetch --> Observations : send or ctx.Done()
Observations --> Normalize
Normalize --> Normalized : send or ctx.Done()
Normalized --> Reducer : deterministic order
Reducer --> Writer : bounded batches
Writer --> Catalog
Reducer --> ObjectWriter : canonical snapshot
ObjectWriter --> Storage : immutable object
ObjectWriter --> Signer : object hash
Signer --> Activator : signed manifest
Activator --> Catalog : activate in transaction

Owner ..> Fetch : owns, limits, cancels, waits
Owner ..> Normalize : owns, limits, cancels, waits
Owner ..> Writer : owns, limits, cancels, waits
Owner ..> ObjectWriter : owns, limits, cancels, waits
Owner ..> Jobs : heartbeat; retryable or terminal outcome

note bottom of Owner
  First error cancels the shared context.
  Producers stop on ctx.Done(). Only owners close channels.
  Completion waits for every owned goroutine.
end note

note bottom of Reducer
  Parallel work stops here. Reduction remains single-owner
  so the published bytes and hash are reproducible.
end note

legend right
  P, O and N are configuration limits, not unbounded buffers.
  Limits derive from provider policy, CPU, pgxpool,
  object storage and the shutdown deadline.
endlegend
@enduml
